NAME
    mv — Move files and directories, because sometimes things belong
    somewhere else.

SYNOPSIS
    mv <src> <dst>

DESCRIPTION
    The mv command moves a file or directory from a source path to a
    destination path.

    Unlike cp, the original file or directory is removed from its
    original location after being moved.

    The destination can be a file or directory depending on the source
    and destination paths.

USAGE
    > mv <src> <dst>       # Moves the source to the destination.

FUN FACTS
    - mv stands for "move", not "maximum velocity".
    - Moving a file does not create a second copy.
    - Directories can be moved just like files.
    - If you move something and forget where you put it, ls and pwd
    are your new best friends.

EXAMPLES
    > mv notes.txt documents/notes.txt
        Moves notes.txt into the documents directory.

    > mv old.txt new.txt
        Moves old.txt and gives it the name new.txt.

    > mv documents old_documents
        Moves the documents directory to old_documents.

    > mv /home/user/file.txt /home/user/backup/file.txt
        Moves file.txt to the backup directory.

SEE ALSO
    help(1), ls(1), cp(1), rm(1), cd(1), man(1)

    Remember, mv does not make a copy.
    It simply gives your file a new place to live.

